<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhou Enlai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1976">1976</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhou Enlai was the first premier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, serving from 1949 until his death in 1976, and its foreign minister until 1958. A veteran of the Long March, he represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Conference of 1954 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung-conference">Bandung Conference</a> of 1955 and helped arrange the rapprochement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1971–72.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai.jpg" alt="Zhou Enlai" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: President (1969-1974 : Nixon). White House Photo Office. 1969-1974 … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Premier_Chou_En-Lai_in_the_Great_Hall_of_the_People%27s_Banquet_Hall_-_DPLA_-_a40021a8100507bc7a244420481bf488.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Zhou was born on 5 March 1898 in Huai'an, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>, and studied at the Nankai Middle School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a>. His Chinese biography makes him a leader of the Tianjin student protests of the May Fourth Movement, but many modern scholars think it unlikely that he took part. In 1924 he returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whampoa-military-academy">Whampoa Military Academy</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> as its chief political officer, and he married Deng Yingchao on 8 August 1925.</p><p>After the split between Nationalists and Communists in 1927 he went underground. From 1929 he built a network of Communist cells in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, changing residence at least once a month. In the Jiangxi Soviet he reorganised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, and when the Communists abandoned it he organised the logistics of the withdrawal that became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a>. In 1936 he helped bring about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian-incident">Xi'an Incident</a> through contact with Zhang Xueliang. During the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> he led the Communist liaison office at the Nationalist capital, first at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuhan">Wuhan</a> and then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> was proclaimed on 1 October 1949, Zhou became Premier and Minister of Foreign Affairs, holding the second post until 1958. He became the architect of early foreign policy. He cultivated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> and helped formalise the Five Principles of Peaceful Coexistence from the 1954 Sino-Indian Agreement. At the Geneva Conference of 1954 he was credited with helping the powers reach the agreement ending the Indochina War, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung-conference">Bandung Conference</a> of 1955 he spoke for peace and the abolition of nuclear arms.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> he was the main force keeping the government and economy running. He tried to protect scientists, academics and artists, though he could not shelter most officials in the institutions he controlled. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lin-biao">Lin Biao</a> he was elected first-ranking Vice Chairman of the Party in 1973.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Zhou withdrew from public life after 13 January 1975 and died on 8 January 1976. He had willed that his ashes be scattered over the hills and rivers of his hometown, so there was no burial ceremony. After a single memorial on 15 January, further public mourning was prohibited. On 4 April 1976 thousands gathered at the Monument to the People's Heroes in Tiananmen Square to commemorate him, and some slogans attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gang-of-four">Gang of Four</a> and even Mao.</p><p>He has been remembered as a skilled negotiator and a pragmatic statesman. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> held that without him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> might have been worse, but might not have lasted as long.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhou Enlai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cambodia had a Chinese minority of about 300,000, whom Premier <b>Zhou Enlai</b> asked in 1956 to stay out of politics and to consider Cambodian citizenship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-cambodia-1953-1970">Kingdom of Cambodia (1953-1970) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its intervention in Korea established it as a military power, and at the Bandung Conference of 1955 <b>Zhou Enlai</b> presented China as a partner of newly independent states in Asia and Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Chen Duxiu and Li Dazhao, leaders of the movement, were the most influential promoters of Marxism in China in these years and became founders of the Chinese Communist Party in 1921; others drawn to communist ideas included Chen Tanqiu and <b>Zhou Enlai</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/may-fourth-movement">May Fourth Movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The Five Principles of Peaceful Coexistence, or Panchsheel, set out by <b>Zhou Enlai</b> and Nehru for Sino-Indian relations, later served as the movement’s basis: mutual respect for territorial integrity and sovereignty, non-aggression, non-interference in domestic affairs, equality and mutual benefit, and peaceful coexistence.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Cultural Revolution Premier <b>Zhou Enlai</b> sent an army battalion to guard it.</blockquote><a href="/work/forbidden-city">Forbidden City · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1966 it escaped damage during the Cultural Revolution through the personal intervention of <b>Zhou Enlai</b>, then Premier of the People’s Republic of China.</blockquote><a href="/work/potala-palace">Potala Palace · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Huai'an District, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.51,119.14&amp;year=1948" aria-label="Huai'an District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7594 948 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zhou Enlai"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8044 1218h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8044 1218h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.51,119.14&amp;year=1948">What was here in 1948? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
